The finding's premise is contradicted by the code it cites. In src/lib/bunny-missions/manager.server.ts, deviceAgenticRefusal (lines 2043-2046) explicitly skips the empty-params remoteExecutionRefusal check for manifests whose id is "filesystem.write" or "filesystem.delete":

  if (manifest.id !== "filesystem.write" && manifest.id !== "filesystem.delete" && remoteExecutionRefusal(manifest.id, {}, manifest.risk)) return DESKTOP_REQUIRED;

The exclusion keys on manifest.id, not on the capability string, so a filesystem.write or filesystem.delete manifest reached through any capability (exact id or a "filesystem.*" prefix via manifestsFor) is never passed to remoteExecutionRefusal with {}. The comment at lines 2043-2044 states this is deliberate: writes and deletes are judged per path at execution under the remote envelope. Re-consent for a reviewed bounded-file-write Agent therefore is not refused by this path, which is the opposite of the reported DESKTOP_REQUIRED outcome. The finding's probe of remoteExecutionRefusal("filesystem.write", {}, "WRITE") returning a refusal is accurate for that function in isolation, but that function is not reached for these ids from deviceAgenticRefusal. The reported line 2041 is the deviceApprovalRefusal loop, not the remoteExecutionRefusal call (2046). I did not run a live probe; the verdict rests on reading the exclusion, which is unambiguous. I made no repo edits and ran no tests.
